\layout Section

Coordinate Systems and Flux-Surface Geometry 
\begin_inset LatexCommand \label{coordi}

\end_inset 


\layout Standard

Three different coordinates system are considered here.
\layout Itemize

First, the toroidal coordinate system 
\begin_inset Formula \( \left( R,Z,\phi \right) \)
\end_inset 

 with local orthogonal vectors 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{eqnarray} 
\widehat{R} &=&\nabla R \\
\widehat{Z} &=&\nabla Z \\
\widehat{\phi } &=&R\nabla \phi 
\end{eqnarray}
\end_inset 

 This coordinate system conserves the largest generality in the magnetic
 geometry
\layout Itemize

Second, the poloidal (polar) coordinate system 
\begin_inset Formula \( \left( r,\theta ,\phi
\right) \)
\end_inset 

, centered around the magnetic axis 
\begin_inset Formula \( \left( R_{0},Z_{0}\right) \)
\end_inset 

 defined as the position of an extremum of 
\begin_inset Formula \( \psi \)
\end_inset 

 (can be arbitrarily chosen as 
\begin_inset Formula \( \psi =0\)
\end_inset 

), and with local orthogonal vectors 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{eqnarray} 
\widehat{r} &=&\nabla r \\
\widehat{\theta } &=&r\nabla \theta  \\
\widehat{\phi } &=&R\nabla \phi 
\end{eqnarray}
\end_inset 

 and the relations 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{eqnarray} 
R &=&R_{0}+r\cos \theta  \\
Z &=&Z_{0}+r\sin \theta 
\end{eqnarray}
\end_inset 

 which can be inverted as 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{eqnarray} 
r &=&\sqrt{\left( R-R_{0}\right) ^{2}+\left( Z-Z_{0}\right) ^{2}} \\
\theta  &=&\arctan \left( \frac{Z-Z_{0}}{R-R_{0}}\right) +\pi \text{sign}\left( R-R_{0}\right)   \label{theta}
\end{eqnarray}
\end_inset 


\layout Itemize

Third, the flux coordinate system 
\begin_inset Formula \( \left( \psi ,s,\phi \right) \)
\end_inset 

 is the natural system for Fokker-Planck simulations, since the particles
 are essentially confined to a given flux surface 
\begin_inset Formula \( \psi \)
\end_inset 

.
 The distance 
\begin_inset Formula \( s\)
\end_inset 

 is the length along the poloidal magnetic field lines.
 We can choose its origin as being at the position of minimum 
\begin_inset Formula \( B\)
\end_inset 

-field amplitude within a flux-surface. 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation} 
B\left( \psi ,s\equiv 0\right) =\min \left\{ B\left( \psi ,s\right) \right\}
=B_{\min }\left( \psi \right) 
\end{equation}
\end_inset 

 The direction is counter-clockwise and the limits 
\begin_inset Formula \( s_{\min }\)
\end_inset 

 and 
\begin_inset Formula \( s_{\max }\)
\end_inset 

 are set at the position of maximum magnetic field
\begin_deeper 
\layout Standard


\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation} 
B\left( \psi ,s\equiv s_{\min }\right) =B\left( \psi ,s\equiv s_{\max
}\right) =\max \left\{ B\left( \psi ,s\right) \right\} =B_{\max }\left( \psi
\right)   \label{bmax}
\end{equation}
\end_inset 

 The local orthogonal vectors are 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{eqnarray} 
\widehat{\psi } &=&\frac{\nabla \psi }{\left| \nabla \psi \right| }
\label{psih} \\
\widehat{s} &=&\frac{\nabla \phi \times \nabla \psi }{\left| \nabla \psi
\right| \left| \nabla \phi \right| }=-\widehat{b}_{p} \\
\widehat{\phi } &=&R\nabla \phi   \label{phih}
\end{eqnarray}
\end_inset 

 where 
\begin_inset Formula \( \widehat{b}_{p}\)
\end_inset 

 is the unit vector in the direction of the poloidal magnetic field.
\end_deeper 
\layout Itemize

An alternative to the previous system may be easier to implement numerically
 for the calculation of the bounce coefficients.
 This is the system 
\begin_inset Formula \( \left( \psi ,\theta ,\phi \right) \)
\end_inset 

.
 The 
\begin_inset Formula \( \theta \)
\end_inset 

 grid is defined in (
\begin_inset LatexCommand \ref{theta}

\end_inset 

).
 We also define, for geometrical purposes, a flux-function 
\begin_inset Formula \( \rho \left( \psi \right) \)
\end_inset 

 which coincides with the normalized radius on the horizontal LFS midplane.
 Indeed, in an axisymmetric system, using the functions 
\begin_inset Formula \( R\left( \psi ,\theta \right) \)
\end_inset 

 and 
\begin_inset Formula \( Z\left( \psi ,\theta \right) \)
\end_inset 

, we define 
\begin_inset Formula \( \rho \left( \psi \right) \)
\end_inset 

 as 
\begin_inset Formula \[ 
\rho \left( \psi \right) =\frac{R\left( \psi ,0\right) -R_{0}}{R_{\max
}-R_{0}}
\]
\end_inset 

 where 
\begin_inset Formula \( R_{\max }=R\left( \psi _{\max },0\right) \)
\end_inset 

 is the value of 
\begin_inset Formula \( R\)
\end_inset 

 on the separatrix as it crosses the midplane.
 The local orthogonal vectors are the same as (
\begin_inset LatexCommand \ref{psih}

\end_inset 

-
\begin_inset LatexCommand \ref{phih}

\end_inset 

).
 The 2-D outputs from the axisymmetric equilibrium code HELENA
\latex latex 

\backslash  
\latex default 
are given on the 
\begin_inset Formula \( \left( \psi ,\theta \right) \)
\end_inset 

 grid.
 The system 
\begin_inset Formula \( \left( \psi ,\theta ,\phi \right) \)
\end_inset 

 will be used from now on. 
\layout Section

Transit or Bounce Time
\layout Subsection

Normalized Expression
\layout Standard

The transit, or bounce time, is defined as the time for a passing particle
 to complete a full orbit in the poloidal plane, and for a trapped particle
 to complete half a bounce period.
 Note that this is possible only in the approximation of zero banana width.
 Otherwise, the bounce motion would be no longer symmetric in the forth
 and back motions, and both would need to be acounted for.
 We define then 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{eqnarray} 
\tau _{b}\left( \psi \right)  &=&\int_{s_{\min }}^{s_{\max }}\frac{ds}{\left| v_{s}\right| } \\
&=&\int_{s_{\min }}^{s_{\max }}\frac{ds}{\left| v_{\Vert }\right| }\frac{B}{B_{P}}
\end{eqnarray}
\end_inset 

 where 
\begin_inset Formula \( s_{\min }\)
\end_inset 

 and 
\begin_inset Formula \( s_{\max }\)
\end_inset 

 are defined in (
\begin_inset LatexCommand \ref{bmax}

\end_inset 

) for passing electrons, and are the positions, along the field lines, of
 turning points for trapped electrons.
\layout Standard

Using the metric dependence of 
\begin_inset Formula \( ds\)
\end_inset 

 versus 
\begin_inset Formula \( d\theta \)
\end_inset 


\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation} 
ds=\sqrt{g_{22}}d\theta 
\end{equation}
\end_inset 

 and introducing the pitch-angle cosine 
\begin_inset Formula \( \xi \)
\end_inset 


\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation} 
v_{\Vert }=v\xi 
\end{equation}
\end_inset 

 we find 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation} 
\tau _{b}\left( \psi \right) =\frac{2\pi }{v\left| \xi _{0}\right| }\int_{\theta _{\min }}^{\theta _{\max }}\frac{d\theta }{2\pi }\sqrt{g_{22}}\frac{\xi _{0}}{\xi }\frac{B}{B_{P}}
\end{equation}
\end_inset 

 where 
\begin_inset Formula \( \xi _{0}\)
\end_inset 

 is the pitch angle cosine at the position 
\begin_inset Formula \( \theta _{0}\)
\end_inset 

 of minimum 
\begin_inset Formula \( B\)
\end_inset 

-field 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation} 
\theta _{0}\equiv \theta \left( B=B_{\min }\left( \psi \right) \right) 
\end{equation}
\end_inset 

 and the limits
\begin_inset Formula \( \,\,\theta _{\min }\)
\end_inset 

 and 
\begin_inset Formula \( \theta _{\max }\)
\end_inset 

 will be calculated in the next subsection.
\layout Standard

The bounce time can be normalized as such: 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation} 
\tau _{b}\left( \psi ,\xi _{0}\right) =\frac{2\pi a\rho \left( \psi \right) 
}{v\left| \xi _{0}\right| }\sqrt{1+\left( \frac{R_{0}q\left( \psi \right) }{a\rho \left( \psi \right) }\right) ^{2}}\lambda \left( \psi ,\xi _{0}\right) 
\end{equation}
\end_inset 

 with 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation} 
\lambda \left( \psi ,\xi _{0}\right) =\left[ 1+\left( \frac{R_{0}q\left(
\psi \right) }{a\rho \left( \psi \right) }\right) ^{2}\right]
^{-1/2}\int_{\theta _{\min }}^{\theta _{\max }}\frac{d\theta }{2\pi }\frac{\sqrt{g_{22}}}{a\rho \left( \psi \right) }\frac{\xi _{0}}{\xi }\frac{B}{B_{P}}  \label{lambda0}
\end{equation}
\end_inset 


\layout Standard

This complicated-looking expression is defined such as to take a very simple
 intuitive form for the case of circular concentric flux-surfaces.
 Indeed, in the latter case, we will have 
\begin_inset Formula \( \lambda \left( \psi ,\pm 1\right) =1\)
\end_inset 

, which means that the bounce time is normalized to the transit time of
 particles that exhibit only parallel velocity.
 This properties, and more generally the analytical calculation for circular
 concentric flux-surfaces, will be addressed in Section 
\begin_inset LatexCommand \ref{circonc}

\end_inset 

.
\layout Standard

The contra-variant metric element 
\begin_inset Formula \( g_{22}\)
\end_inset 

 is given in the 
\begin_inset Formula \( \left( \psi
,\theta ,\phi \right) \)
\end_inset 

 system by 
\begin_inset Formula \[ 
g_{22}=\left| J\nabla \psi \times \nabla \phi \right| ^{2}=J^{2}\left|
\nabla \psi \right| ^{2}\left| \nabla \phi \right| ^{2} 
\]
\end_inset 

 with the Jacobian being 
\begin_inset Formula \[ 
J=\left| \nabla \psi \times \nabla \theta \cdot \nabla \phi \right| ^{-1} 
\]
\end_inset 

 so that 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{eqnarray*} 
\sqrt{g_{22}} &=&J\left| \nabla \psi \right| \left| \nabla \phi \right| \\
&=&\frac{\left| \nabla \psi \right| \left| \nabla \phi \right| }{\left|
\nabla \psi \times \nabla \theta \cdot \nabla \phi \right| } \\
&=&\frac{\left| \nabla \psi \right| \left| \nabla \phi \right| }{\left|
\nabla \psi \cdot \nabla \theta \times \nabla \phi \right| } \\
&=&\frac{r}{\left| \widehat{\psi }\cdot \widehat{r}\right| }
\end{eqnarray*}
\end_inset 

 where the unit vectors were defined in Section 
\begin_inset LatexCommand \ref{coordi}

\end_inset 

 and where we used 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{eqnarray*} 
\nabla \theta &=&\frac{\widehat{\theta }}{r} \\
\nabla \phi &=&\left| \nabla \phi \right| \widehat{\phi } \\
\nabla \psi &=&\left| \nabla \psi \right| \widehat{\psi } \\
\widehat{r} &=&\widehat{\theta }\times \widehat{\phi }
\end{eqnarray*}
\end_inset 


\layout Standard

Consequently, the normalized bounce time takes the form 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation} 
\lambda \left( \psi ,\xi _{0}\right) =\frac{1}{a\rho \left( \psi \right) }\left[ 1+\left( \frac{R_{0}q\left( \psi \right) }{a\rho \left( \psi \right) }\right) ^{2}\right] ^{-1/2}\int_{\theta _{\min }}^{\theta _{\max }}\frac{d\theta }{2\pi }\frac{r}{\left| \widehat{\psi }\cdot \widehat{r}\right| }\frac{\xi _{0}}{\xi }\frac{B}{B_{P}}  \label{lambda}
\end{equation}
\end_inset 


\layout Subsection

Particle Motion in the Magnetic Field
\layout Standard

The particle motion along the magnetic field lines exhibits one constant
 of the motions, the energy (or the total momentum), and an adiabatic invariant,
 the magnetic moment.
 They are given by the equations 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{eqnarray} 
p^{2} &=&p_{\bot }^{2}+p_{\Vert }^{2} \\
\mu &=&\frac{p_{\bot }^{2}}{2mB}
\end{eqnarray}
\end_inset 

 such that, as a function of the moment component 
\begin_inset Formula \( \left( p_{\Vert 0},p_{\bot
0}\right) \)
\end_inset 

 at the location 
\begin_inset Formula \( \theta _{0}\)
\end_inset 

 of minimum 
\begin_inset Formula \( B\)
\end_inset 

-field, we have 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{eqnarray} 
p_{\bot }^{2}+p_{\Vert }^{2} &=&p_{\bot 0}^{2}+p_{\Vert 0}^{2}  \label{mom}
\\
\frac{p_{\bot }^{2}}{B\left( \psi ,\theta \right) } &=&\frac{p_{\bot 0}^{2}}{B_{\min }\left( \psi \right) }  \label{mhu}
\end{eqnarray}
\end_inset 


\layout Subsubsection

Pitch-angle Cosine 
\begin_inset Formula \( \xi \)
\end_inset 


\layout Standard

The pitch-angle cosine is defined as 
\begin_inset Formula \[ 
\xi =\frac{p_{\Vert }}{p} 
\]
\end_inset 

 so that 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{eqnarray*} 
p_{\Vert } &=&p\xi \\
p_{\bot } &=&p\sqrt{1-\xi ^{2}}
\end{eqnarray*}
\end_inset 

 and the system (
\begin_inset LatexCommand \ref{mom}

\end_inset 

-
\begin_inset LatexCommand \ref{mhu}

\end_inset 

) becomes 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{eqnarray} 
p^{2} &=&p_{0}^{2} \\
\frac{1-\xi ^{2}}{B\left( \psi ,\theta \right) } &=&\frac{1-\xi _{0}^{2}}{B_{\min }\left( \psi \right) }
\end{eqnarray}
\end_inset 


\layout Standard

We get an expression for 
\begin_inset Formula \( \xi \)
\end_inset 

 as a function of 
\begin_inset Formula \( \xi _{0}\)
\end_inset 

: 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation} 
\xi \left( \psi ,\theta ,\xi _{0}\right) = \text{sign}\left( \xi _{0}\right) 
\sqrt{1-\frac{B\left( \psi ,\theta \right) }{B_{\min }\left( \psi \right) }\left( 1-\xi _{0}^{2}\right) }  \label{xi}
\end{equation}
\end_inset 


\layout Subsubsection

Trapping Condition
\layout Standard

The trapping condition is given by 
\begin_inset Formula \( \left| \xi _{0}\right| <\xi _{0T}\left(
\psi \right) \)
\end_inset 

 , where 
\begin_inset Formula \( \xi _{0T}\left( \psi \right) \)
\end_inset 

 is the pitch angle, defined at the minimum 
\begin_inset Formula \( B_{\min }\left( \psi \right) \)
\end_inset 

 on a given flux-surface, such that the parallel velocity of the particle
 vanishes at the maximum 
\begin_inset Formula \( B_{\max }\left( \psi \right) \)
\end_inset 

.
 An expression for 
\begin_inset Formula \( \xi
_{0T}\left( \psi \right) \)
\end_inset 

 can then be obtained from (
\begin_inset LatexCommand \ref{xi}

\end_inset 

): setting 
\begin_inset Formula \( \xi \left( \xi _{0T},B=B_{\max }\left( \psi \right) \right) =0\)
\end_inset 

, we get 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation} 
\xi _{0T}^{2}\left( \psi \right) =1-\frac{B_{\min }\left( \psi \right) }{B_{\max }\left( \psi \right) }
\end{equation}
\end_inset 


\layout Subsubsection

Turning Points 
\begin_inset Formula \( \theta _{\min }\)
\end_inset 

 and 
\begin_inset Formula \( \theta _{\max }\)
\end_inset 


\layout Standard

The turning points are 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation} 
\theta _{\min }\left( \psi ,\xi _{0}\right) =\left| 
\begin{array}{cc}
-\pi & \text{for passing particles} \\ 
\theta _{T\min } & \text{for trapped particles}
\end{array}
\right.
\end{equation}
\end_inset 


\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation} 
\theta _{\max }\left( \psi ,\xi _{0}\right) =\left| 
\begin{array}{cc}
\pi & \text{for passing particles} \\ 
\theta _{T\max } & \text{for trapped particles}
\end{array}
\right.
\end{equation}
\end_inset 


\layout Standard

We can determine the turning angles 
\begin_inset Formula \( \theta _{T\min }\left( \psi ,\xi
_{0}\right) \)
\end_inset 

 and 
\begin_inset Formula \( \theta _{T\max }\left( \psi ,\xi _{0}\right) \)
\end_inset 

 as the position where 
\begin_inset Formula \( \xi \left( \psi ,\theta ,\xi _{0}\right) =0\)
\end_inset 

.
 At this position, we have 
\begin_inset Formula \( B=B_{b}\left( \psi ,\xi _{0}\right) \)
\end_inset 

, where 
\begin_inset Formula \( B_{b}\left(
\psi ,\xi _{0}\right) \)
\end_inset 

 is then given by (
\begin_inset LatexCommand \ref{xi}

\end_inset 

) 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation} 
B_{b}\left( \psi ,\xi _{0}\right) =\frac{B_{\min }\left( \psi \right) }{1-\xi _{0}^{2}}
\end{equation}
\end_inset 

 so that 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{eqnarray} 
\theta _{T\min }\left( \psi ,\xi _{0}\right)  &=&\theta \left( B=B_{b}\left|
\theta <\theta _{0}\right. \right) \;[2\pi ]  \label{thetamin} \\
\theta _{T\max }\left( \psi ,\xi _{0}\right)  &=&\theta \left( B=B_{b}\left|
\theta >\theta _{0}\right. \right) \;[2\pi ]
\end{eqnarray}
\end_inset 


\layout Subsection

Calculation of 
\begin_inset Formula \( \lambda \left( \psi ,\xi _{0}\right) \)
\end_inset 

 From the Output Data of Equilibrium Codes
\layout Standard

The numerical calculation of 
\begin_inset Formula \( \lambda \left( \psi ,\xi _{0}\right) \)
\end_inset 

 can be carried from the output of equilibrium codes such as HELENA.
 These data are assumed to be the parametrization of the flux-surfaces 
\begin_inset Formula \( R\left( \psi ,\theta
\right) \)
\end_inset 

 and 
\begin_inset Formula \( Z\left( \psi ,\theta \right) \)
\end_inset 

, and the three components of the magnetic field 
\begin_inset Formula \( B_{R}\left( \psi ,\theta \right) \)
\end_inset 

, 
\begin_inset Formula \( B_{Z}\left( \psi
,\theta \right) \)
\end_inset 

 and 
\begin_inset Formula \( B_{\phi }\left( \psi ,\theta \right) \)
\end_inset 

.
 From these components we derive directly the toroidal and poloidal components
 of the field, as well as the total field: 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{eqnarray} 
B_{T}\left( \psi ,\theta \right) &=&\left| B_{\phi }\left( \psi ,\theta
\right) \right|  \nonumber \\
B_{P}\left( \psi ,\theta \right) &=&\sqrt{B_{R}^{2}\left( \psi ,\theta
\right) +B_{Z}^{2}\left( \psi ,\theta \right) }  \nonumber \\
B\left( \psi ,\theta \right) &=&\sqrt{B_{T}^{2}\left( \psi ,\theta \right)
+B_{P}^{2}\left( \psi ,\theta \right) }  \label{Btot}
\end{eqnarray}
\end_inset 


\layout Standard

We also have an expression for 
\begin_inset Formula \( r\)
\end_inset 

 
\begin_inset Formula \[ 
r\left( \psi ,\theta \right) =\sqrt{\left( R\left( \psi ,\theta \right)
-R_{0}\right) ^{2}+\left( Z\left( \psi ,\theta \right) -Z_{0}\right) ^{2}}
\]
\end_inset 

 and, using 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{eqnarray} 
\widehat{r} &=&\left( \widehat{r}\cdot \widehat{R}\right) \widehat{R}+\left( 
\widehat{r}\cdot \widehat{Z}\right) \widehat{Z} \\
&=&\left( \frac{R\left( \psi ,\theta \right) -R_{0}}{r}\right) \widehat{R}+\left( \frac{Z\left( \psi ,\theta \right) -Z_{0}}{r}\right) \widehat{Z}
\end{eqnarray}
\end_inset 

 we get an expression for the scalar product 
\begin_inset Formula \[ 
\widehat{\psi }\cdot \widehat{r}=\frac{\left( \nabla \psi \cdot \widehat{R}\right) \left( R\left( \psi ,\theta \right) -R_{0}\right) +\left( \nabla
\psi \cdot \widehat{Z}\right) \left( Z\left( \psi ,\theta \right)
-Z_{0}\right) }{r\left| \nabla \psi \right| }
\]
\end_inset 


\layout Standard

In a toroidal axisymmetric geometry, the magnetic field can be expressed
 generally as 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation} 
\mathbf{B}=I\left( \psi \right) \nabla \phi +\nabla \psi \times \nabla \phi
\end{equation}
\end_inset 

 so that 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{eqnarray*} 
B_{T} &=&\left| I\left( \psi \right) \right| \left| \nabla \phi \right| =\frac{\left| I\left( \psi \right) \right| }{R} \\
B_{P} &=&\left| \nabla \psi \right| \left| \nabla \phi \right| =\frac{\left|
\nabla \psi \right| }{R}
\end{eqnarray*}
\end_inset 


\layout Standard

We also have 
\begin_inset Formula \[ 
\mathbf{B}_{P}=\nabla \psi \times \nabla \phi 
\]
\end_inset 

 and therefore 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{eqnarray*} 
\nabla \phi \times \mathbf{B}_{P} &=&\nabla \phi \times \left( \nabla \psi
\times \nabla \phi \right) \\
&=&\left| \nabla \phi \right| ^{2}\nabla \psi
\end{eqnarray*}
\end_inset 

 so that 
\begin_inset Formula \[ 
\nabla \psi =\frac{\nabla \phi \times \mathbf{B}_{P}}{\left| \nabla \phi
\right| ^{2}}=R\widehat{\phi }\times \mathbf{B}_{P} 
\]
\end_inset 

 and we have the projections 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{eqnarray*} 
\left( \nabla \psi \cdot \widehat{R}\right) &=&R\widehat{R}\cdot \widehat{\phi }\times \mathbf{B}_{P}=-RB_{Z} \\
\left( \nabla \psi \cdot \widehat{Z}\right) &=&R\widehat{Z}\cdot \widehat{\phi }\times \mathbf{B}_{P}=RB_{R}
\end{eqnarray*}
\end_inset 


\layout Standard

Finally, the expression for the normalized bounce time becomes 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation} 
\lambda \left( \psi ,\xi _{0}\right) =\frac{1}{a\rho }\left[ 1+\left( \frac{R_{0}q}{a\rho }\right) ^{2}\right] ^{-1/2}\int_{\theta _{\min }}^{\theta
_{\max }}\frac{d\theta }{2\pi }\frac{B\left[ \left( R-R_{0}\right)
^{2}+\left( Z-Z_{0}\right) ^{2}\right] }{\left| B_{R}\left( Z-Z_{0}\right)
-B_{Z}\left( R-R_{0}\right) \right| }\frac{\xi _{0}}{\xi }
\end{equation}
\end_inset 

 where 
\begin_inset Formula \( R,Z,B_{R},B_{Z}\)
\end_inset 

 and 
\begin_inset Formula \( B\)
\end_inset 

 are functions of 
\begin_inset Formula \( \left( \psi ,\theta
\right) \)
\end_inset 

, 
\begin_inset Formula \( \rho \)
\end_inset 

 and 
\begin_inset Formula \( q\)
\end_inset 

 are functions of 
\begin_inset Formula \( \psi \)
\end_inset 

, and 
\begin_inset Formula \( \xi \)
\end_inset 

 is a function of 
\begin_inset Formula \( \left( \psi ,\theta ,\xi _{0}\right) \)
\end_inset 

 given by (
\begin_inset LatexCommand \ref{xi}

\end_inset 

) 
\begin_inset Formula \[ 
\xi \left( \psi ,\theta ,\xi _{0}\right) = \text{sign}\left( \xi _{0}\right) 
\sqrt{1-\frac{B\left( \psi ,\theta \right) }{B_{\min }\left( \psi \right) }\left( 1-\xi _{0}^{2}\right) } 
\]
\end_inset 


\layout Standard

We recall 
\begin_inset Formula \[ 
\rho \left( \psi \right) =\frac{R\left( \psi ,0\right) -R_{0}}{a}
\]
\end_inset 

 so that we get filly the expression 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation} 
\lambda \left( \psi ,\xi _{0}\right) =\frac{1}{R\left( \psi ,0\right) -R_{0}}\left[ 1+\left( \frac{R_{0}q}{R\left( \psi ,0\right) -R_{0}}\right)
^{2}\right] ^{-1/2}\int_{\theta _{\min }}^{\theta _{\max }}\frac{d\theta }{2\pi }\frac{B\left[ \left( R-R_{0}\right) ^{2}+\left( Z-Z_{0}\right)
^{2}\right] }{\left| B_{R}\left( Z-Z_{0}\right) -B_{Z}\left( R-R_{0}\right)
\right| }\frac{\xi _{0}}{\xi }  \label{lambda1}
\end{equation}
\end_inset 

 which will be used to calculate 
\begin_inset Formula \( \lambda \)
\end_inset 

 numerically.
\layout Section

Bounce Average
\layout Standard

In order to reduce the dimension of kinetic equations, it is important to
 define an average over the poloidal motion, which anihilates the term that
 accounts for the time evolution of the variations of the distribution function
 along the field lines.
 The natural average is 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation} 
\left\{ \mathcal{A}\right\} =\frac{1}{\tau _{b}}\int_{s_{\min }}^{s_{\max }}\frac{ds}{\left| v_{s}\right| }\mathcal{A}
\end{equation}
\end_inset 


\layout Standard

It can be rewritten in terms of the normalized bounce time 
\begin_inset Formula \( \lambda \)
\end_inset 

 (
\begin_inset LatexCommand \ref{lambda0}

\end_inset 

) 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation} 
\left\{ \mathcal{A}\right\} =\frac{1}{\lambda }\left[ 1+\left( \frac{R_{0}q\left( \psi \right) }{a\rho \left( \psi \right) }\right) ^{2}\right]
^{-1/2}\int_{\theta _{\min }}^{\theta _{\max }}\frac{d\theta }{2\pi }\frac{\sqrt{g_{22}}}{a\rho \left( \psi \right) }\frac{\xi _{0}}{\xi }\frac{B}{B_{P}}\mathcal{A}
\end{equation}
\end_inset 


\layout Standard

Another expression uses the output data from equilibrium codes.
 Following the work in the previous section, we find 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation} 
\left\{ \mathcal{A}\right\} =\frac{1}{\lambda }\frac{1}{R\left( \psi
,0\right) -R_{0}}\left[ 1+\left( \frac{R_{0}q}{R\left( \psi ,0\right) -R_{0}}\right) ^{2}\right] ^{-1/2}\int_{\theta _{\min }}^{\theta _{\max }}\frac{d\theta }{2\pi }\frac{B\left[ \left( R-R_{0}\right) ^{2}+\left(
Z-Z_{0}\right) ^{2}\right] }{\left| B_{R}\left( Z-Z_{0}\right) -B_{Z}\left(
R-R_{0}\right) \right| }\frac{\xi _{0}}{\xi }\mathcal{A}
\end{equation}
\end_inset 

 or equivalently 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation} 
\left\{ \mathcal{A}\right\} =\left[ \int_{\theta _{\min }}^{\theta _{\max }}\frac{d\theta }{2\pi }\frac{B\left[ \left( R-R_{0}\right) ^{2}+\left(
Z-Z_{0}\right) ^{2}\right] }{\left| B_{R}\left( Z-Z_{0}\right) -B_{Z}\left(
R-R_{0}\right) \right| }\frac{\xi _{0}}{\xi }\right] ^{-1}\int_{\theta
_{\min }}^{\theta _{\max }}\frac{d\theta }{2\pi }\frac{B\left[ \left(
R-R_{0}\right) ^{2}+\left( Z-Z_{0}\right) ^{2}\right] }{\left| B_{R}\left(
Z-Z_{0}\right) -B_{Z}\left( R-R_{0}\right) \right| }\frac{\xi _{0}}{\xi }\mathcal{A}
\end{equation}
\end_inset 


\layout Section

Numerical Integration over 
\begin_inset Formula \( \theta \)
\end_inset 


\layout Standard

The numerical calculation of bounce coefficients requires an integration
 over 
\begin_inset Formula \( \theta \)
\end_inset 

 which can be expressed symbolically as 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation} 
I\left( \psi ,\xi _{0}\right) =\int_{\theta _{\min }}^{\theta _{\max }}\frac{d\theta }{2\pi }F\left( \psi ,\theta ,\xi _{0};B_{R},B_{Z},B_{\phi
},R,Z\right) 
\end{equation}
\end_inset 

 where 
\begin_inset Formula \( B_{R},B_{Z},B_{\phi },R,Z\)
\end_inset 

 are functions of 
\begin_inset Formula \( \left( \psi ,\theta
\right) \)
\end_inset 

.
 They are given on a uniform grid of 
\begin_inset Formula \( N_{\theta }\)
\end_inset 

 points in 
\begin_inset Formula \( \theta \)
\end_inset 


\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation} 
\theta _{j}=\frac{2\pi j}{N_{\theta }-1},\;j=0,1,\cdots ,N_{\theta }-1
\end{equation}
\end_inset 


\layout Subsection

Domain of Integration
\layout Standard

It is very important to account for the entire bounce path of the particle,
 including in particular the tip of banana orbits near 
\begin_inset Formula \( \theta _{T\min }\)
\end_inset 

 and 
\begin_inset Formula \( \theta _{T\max }\)
\end_inset 

.
 The contribution of these banana tips is often larger than the 
\begin_inset Formula \( d\theta =2\pi /(N_{\theta }-1)\)
\end_inset 

 accuracy level, because 
\begin_inset Formula \( F\left(
\theta \right) \)
\end_inset 

 can become very large near the turning points.
 This is true for example in the calculation of 
\begin_inset Formula \( \lambda \)
\end_inset 

, since 
\begin_inset Formula \( F\left( \theta \right)
\sim \)
\end_inset 

 
\begin_inset Formula \( 1/\xi \)
\end_inset 

 and 
\begin_inset Formula \( \xi \rightarrow 0\)
\end_inset 

 at the turning points.
 It is therefore crucial to perform the integration up to 
\begin_inset Formula \( \theta _{T\min }\)
\end_inset 

 and 
\begin_inset Formula \( \theta _{T\max }\)
\end_inset 

.
 However, these boundaries are defined by (
\begin_inset LatexCommand \ref{tehtamin}

\end_inset 

) 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation} 
B\left( \psi ,\theta _{T}\right) =B_{b}\left( \psi ,\xi _{0}\right) =\frac{B_{\min }\left( \psi \right) }{1-\xi _{0}^{2}}
\end{equation}
\end_inset 

 which in general do not coincide with a grid points.
 In order to calculate 
\begin_inset Formula \( \theta _{T}\)
\end_inset 

, we impose that the values of the data 
\begin_inset Formula \( B_{R},B_{Z},B_{\phi
},R,Z\)
\end_inset 

 in 
\begin_inset Formula \( \theta _{T}\)
\end_inset 

 be obtained by linear interpolation, while the value of 
\begin_inset Formula \( B\left( \psi ,\theta _{T}\right) \)
\end_inset 

 is obtained from (
\begin_inset LatexCommand \ref{Btot}

\end_inset 

).
\layout Standard

We consider the magnetic field 
\begin_inset Formula \( B_{b}\left( \psi ,\xi _{0}\right) \)
\end_inset 

 at the turning point 
\begin_inset Formula \( \theta _{T\min }\)
\end_inset 

 to be located between the two (consecutive) values 
\begin_inset Formula \( B_{1}\left( \psi ,\theta _{1}\right) \)
\end_inset 

 and 
\begin_inset Formula \( B_{2}\left( \psi ,\theta
_{2}\right) \)
\end_inset 

 on the 
\begin_inset Formula \( \left( \psi ,\theta \right) \)
\end_inset 

 grid.
 These values are determined from the three components issued by the equilibrium
 code simply by 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{eqnarray*} 
B_{1}\left( \psi ,\theta _{1}\right) &=&\sqrt{B_{R}^{2}\left( \psi ,\theta
_{1}\right) +B_{Z}^{2}\left( \psi ,\theta _{1}\right) +B_{\phi }^{2}\left(
\psi ,\theta _{1}\right) } \\
B_{2}\left( \psi ,\theta _{1}\right) &=&\sqrt{B_{R}^{2}\left( \psi ,\theta
_{2}\right) +B_{Z}^{2}\left( \psi ,\theta _{2}\right) +B_{\phi }^{2}\left(
\psi ,\theta _{2}\right) }
\end{eqnarray*}
\end_inset 


\layout Standard

We choose to define the values of 
\begin_inset Formula \( B_{R}\)
\end_inset 

, 
\begin_inset Formula \( B_{Z}\,\,\)
\end_inset 

and 
\begin_inset Formula \( B_{\phi }\)
\end_inset 

 at the location 
\begin_inset Formula \( \theta _{T}\)
\end_inset 

 by linear interpolation: 
\begin_inset Formula \[ 
B_{i}\left( \psi ,\theta _{T}\right) =B_{i1}+\frac{\left( \theta _{T}-\theta
_{1}\right) }{\left( \theta _{2}-\theta _{1}\right) }\left(
B_{i2}-B_{i1}\right) 
\]
\end_inset 

 where 
\begin_inset Formula \( i=R,Z,\phi \)
\end_inset 

.
 Then, the location 
\begin_inset Formula \( \theta _{T}\)
\end_inset 

 of the turning point can be calculated by requiring that the relation 
\begin_inset Formula \[ 
B_{b}\left( \psi ,\xi _{0}\right) =B\left( \psi ,\theta _{T}\right) =\sqrt{B_{R}^{2}\left( \psi ,\theta _{T}\right) +B_{Z}^{2}\left( \psi ,\theta
_{T}\right) +B_{\phi }^{2}\left( \psi ,\theta _{T}\right) } 
\]
\end_inset 

 be satisfied.
 This implies 
\begin_inset Formula \[ 
B_{R}^{2}\left( \psi ,\theta _{T}\right) +B_{Z}^{2}\left( \psi ,\theta
_{T}\right) +B_{\phi }^{2}\left( \psi ,\theta _{T}\right) =B_{b}^{2}\left(
\psi ,\xi _{0}\right) 
\]
\end_inset 

 and then 
\begin_inset Formula \[ 
\sum_{i=R,Z,\phi }\left[ B_{i1}+\frac{\left( \theta _{T}-\theta _{1}\right) 
}{\left( \theta _{2}-\theta _{1}\right) }\left( B_{i2}-B_{i1}\right) \right]
^{2}-B_{b}^{2}\left( \psi ,\xi _{0}\right) =0 
\]
\end_inset 


\layout Standard

Defining 
\begin_inset Formula \[ 
\alpha =\frac{\left( \theta _{T}-\theta _{1}\right) }{\left( \theta
_{2}-\theta _{1}\right) } 
\]
\end_inset 

 we find 
\begin_inset Formula \[ 
\left[ \sum_{i=R,Z,\phi }\left( B_{i2}-B_{i1}\right) ^{2}\right] \alpha
^{2}+2\left[ \sum_{i=R,Z,\phi }B_{i1}\left( B_{i2}-B_{i1}\right) \right]
\alpha +\sum_{i=R,Z,\phi }B_{i1}^{2}-B_{b}^{2}\left( \psi ,\xi _{0}\right)
=0 
\]
\end_inset 

 which solves as 
\begin_inset Formula \[ 
\alpha =\frac{\pm \sqrt{\left[ \sum_{i}B_{i1}\left( B_{i2}-B_{i1}\right)
\right] ^{2}-\left[ \sum_{i}\left( B_{i2}-B_{i1}\right) ^{2}\right] \left[
\sum_{i}B_{i1}^{2}-B_{b}^{2}\left( \psi ,\xi _{0}\right) \right] }-\sum_{i}B_{i1}\left( B_{i2}-B_{i1}\right) }{\sum_{i}\left(
B_{i2}-B_{i1}\right) ^{2}} 
\]
\end_inset 


\layout Standard

We have 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{eqnarray*} 
&&\sqrt{\left[ \sum_{i}B_{i1}\left( B_{i2}-B_{i1}\right) \right] ^{2}-\left[
\sum_{i}\left( B_{i2}-B_{i1}\right) ^{2}\right] \left[
\sum_{i}B_{i1}^{2}-B_{b}^{2}\left( \psi ,\xi _{0}\right) \right] } \\
&=&\sqrt{\begin{array}{c}
\left( B_{R1}B_{R2}+B_{Z1}B_{Z2}+B_{\phi 1}B_{\phi 2}\right)
^{2}-B_{1}^{2}B_{2}^{2} \\ 
+\left[ B_{2}^{2}+B_{1}^{2}-2\left( B_{R2}B_{R1}+B_{Z2}B_{Z1}+B_{\phi
2}B_{\phi 1}\right) \right] B_{b}^{2}\left( \psi ,\xi _{0}\right)
\end{array}
} \\
&=&\sqrt{\left( Y-B_{b}^{2}\right) ^{2}-\left( B_{b}^{2}-B_{1}^{2}\right)
\left( B_{b}^{2}-B_{2}^{2}\right) }
\end{eqnarray*}
\end_inset 

 with 
\begin_inset Formula \[ 
Y=B_{R1}B_{R2}+B_{Z1}B_{Z2}+B_{\phi 1}B_{\phi 2} 
\]
\end_inset 

 so that 
\begin_inset Formula \[ 
\alpha =\frac{\pm \sqrt{\left( Y-B_{b}^{2}\right) ^{2}-\left(
B_{b}^{2}-B_{1}^{2}\right) \left( B_{b}^{2}-B_{2}^{2}\right) }+B_{1}^{2}-Y}{B_{1}^{2}+B_{2}^{2}-2Y} 
\]
\end_inset 

 and finally 
\begin_inset Formula \[ 
\theta _{T}=\theta _{1}+\left( \theta _{2}-\theta _{1}\right) \frac{\pm 
\sqrt{\left( Y-B_{b}^{2}\right) ^{2}-\left( B_{b}^{2}-B_{1}^{2}\right)
\left( B_{b}^{2}-B_{2}^{2}\right) }+B_{1}^{2}-Y}{B_{1}^{2}+B_{2}^{2}-2Y} 
\]
\end_inset 


\layout Standard

We must choose (numerically) the solution that gives 
\begin_inset Formula \( 0\leq \alpha \leq 1\)
\end_inset 

.
\layout Standard

Note that if the magnetic fields in points 1 and 2 are equal, we have 
\begin_inset Formula \( Y=B_{1}^{2}=B_{2}^{2}=B_{b}^{2}\)
\end_inset 

.
\layout Subsection

Numerical Integration
\layout Standard

Once the two turning points have been added to the 
\begin_inset Formula \( \theta \)
\end_inset 

 grid, now noted 
\begin_inset Formula \( \widetilde{\theta }_{j}\)
\end_inset 

, 
\begin_inset Formula \( j=0,1,2,\cdots N_{\theta }+1\)
\end_inset 

, we define the half grid 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation} 
\overline{\theta }_{k}=\frac{\left( \widetilde{\theta }_{k+1}+\widetilde{\theta }_{k}\right) }{2},\;k=0,1,2,\cdots N_{\theta }
\end{equation}
\end_inset 

 and calculate the discrete function 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation} 
F_{k}=F\left( \psi ,\overline{\theta }_{k},\xi _{0};B_{Rk},B_{Zk},B_{\phi
k},R_{k},Z_{k}\right)
\end{equation}
\end_inset 

 where 
\begin_inset Formula \( B_{Rk},B_{Zk},B_{\phi k},R_{k},Z_{k}\)
\end_inset 

 have been calculated on the grid 
\begin_inset Formula \( \overline{\theta }_{k}\)
\end_inset 

 by linear interpolation, and the step 
\begin_inset Formula \( \overline{d\theta }_{k}\)
\end_inset 

, defined by 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation} 
\overline{d\theta }_{k}=\widetilde{\theta }_{k+1}-\widetilde{\theta }_{k},\;k=0,1,2,\cdots N_{\theta }
\end{equation}
\end_inset 

 so that the integral becomes 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation} 
I\left( \psi ,\xi _{0}\right) =\frac{1}{2\pi }\sum_{k=0}^{N_{\theta }}\overline{d\theta }_{k}F_{k}
\end{equation}
\end_inset 


\layout Section

Analytical Calculation for Idealized Plasmas
\layout Subsection

Circular, Concentric Flux-Surfaces
\begin_inset LatexCommand \label{circonc}

\end_inset 


\layout Subsubsection

Symmetry
\layout Standard

In this case, we have 
\begin_inset Formula \( \psi =\psi \left( r\right) \)
\end_inset 

 and therefore it is easier to work in the 
\begin_inset Formula \( \left( r,\theta ,\phi \right) \)
\end_inset 

 coordinate to account for the symmetry in the problem.
 The normalized radius is 
\begin_inset Formula \[ 
\rho \left( \psi \right) =\frac{r}{a} 
\]
\end_inset 


\layout Standard

We have now 
\begin_inset Formula \[ 
\widehat{\psi }=\widehat{r} 
\]
\end_inset 

 so that 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation} 
\sqrt{g_{22}}=r  \label{g22circ}
\end{equation}
\end_inset 


\layout Subsubsection

Magnetic Field
\layout Standard

The toroidal field is 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation} 
B_{T}\left( \rho ,\theta \right) =\frac{\left| I\left( \rho \right) \right| 
}{R\left( \rho ,\theta \right) }
\end{equation}
\end_inset 

 and the poloidal field is 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation} 
B_{P}\left( \rho ,\theta \right) =\frac{\left| \nabla \psi \left( \rho
\right) \right| }{R\left( \rho ,\theta \right) }  \label{Bpcirc}
\end{equation}
\end_inset 

 where 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation} 
\left| \nabla \psi \left( \rho \right) \right| =\frac{1}{a}\left| \frac{d\psi \left( \rho \right) }{d\rho }\right|
\end{equation}
\end_inset 

 is now only a function of 
\begin_inset Formula \( \rho \)
\end_inset 

; the safety factor can be written as 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation} 
q\left( \rho \right) =\frac{r}{R_{0}}\frac{B_{T}}{B_{P}}=\rho \frac{a}{R_{0}}\frac{\left| I\left( \rho \right) \right| }{\left| \nabla \psi \left( \rho
\right) \right| }
\end{equation}
\end_inset 


\layout Standard

The total field is then 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation} 
B\left( \rho ,\theta \right) =\frac{\sqrt{I^{2}\left( \rho \right) +\left|
\nabla \psi \left( \rho \right) \right| ^{2}}}{R\left( \rho ,\theta \right) }
\end{equation}
\end_inset 

 and can be written as 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation} 
B\left( \rho ,\theta \right) =B_{0}\left( \rho \right) \frac{R_{0}}{R\left(
\rho ,\theta \right) }  \label{Bcirc}
\end{equation}
\end_inset 

 with 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation} 
B_{0}\left( \rho \right) =\sqrt{I^{2}\left( \rho \right) +\left| \nabla \psi
\left( \rho \right) \right| ^{2}}
\end{equation}
\end_inset 


\layout Standard

We have the ratio 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{eqnarray} 
\frac{B}{B_{P}} &=&\frac{\sqrt{I^{2}\left( \rho \right) +\left| \nabla \psi
\left( \rho \right) \right| ^{2}}}{\left| \nabla \psi \left( \rho \right)
\right| }  \label{BsBpcirc} \\
&=&\sqrt{1+\frac{I^{2}\left( \rho \right) }{\left| \nabla \psi \left( \rho
\right) \right| ^{2}}}  \nonumber \\
&=&\sqrt{1+\left( \frac{R_{0}q\left( \rho \right) }{a\rho }\right) ^{2}}
\end{eqnarray}
\end_inset 


\layout Subsubsection

Particle Motion
\layout Standard

Defining 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation} 
\epsilon =\frac{r}{R_{0}}
\end{equation}
\end_inset 

 we find 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{eqnarray} 
R_{\min }\left( r\right) &=&R_{0}-r=R_{0}\left( 1-\epsilon \right) \\
R_{\min }\left( r\right) &=&R_{0}+r=R_{0}\left( 1+\epsilon \right)
\end{eqnarray}
\end_inset 

 so that, using (
\begin_inset LatexCommand \ref{Bcirc}

\end_inset 

) 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{eqnarray} 
B_{\min }\left( r\right) &=&\frac{B_{0}\left( r\right) }{1+\epsilon } \\
B_{\max }\left( r\right) &=&\frac{B_{0}\left( r\right) }{1-\epsilon }
\end{eqnarray}
\end_inset 

 and 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation} 
\xi _{0T}^{2}\left( r\right) =\frac{2\epsilon }{1+\epsilon }
\end{equation}
\end_inset 


\layout Standard

The pitch-angle cosine is then given by (
\begin_inset LatexCommand \ref{xi}

\end_inset 

) 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation} 
\xi \left( r,\theta ,\xi _{0}\right) = \text{sign}\left( \xi _{0}\right) 
\sqrt{1-\frac{1+\epsilon }{1+\epsilon \cos \theta }\left( 1-\xi
_{0}^{2}\right) }
\end{equation}
\end_inset 


\layout Standard

The turning angles are determined by 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation} 
B\left( r,\theta _{T}\right) =B_{b}\left( r,\xi _{0}\right) =\frac{B_{\min
}\left( r\right) }{1-\xi _{0}^{2}}
\end{equation}
\end_inset 

 which gives 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation} 
\frac{B_{0}\left( r\right) }{1+\epsilon \cos \theta _{T}}=\frac{1}{1-\xi
_{0}^{2}}\frac{B_{0}\left( r\right) }{1+\epsilon }
\end{equation}
\end_inset 

 and then 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{eqnarray} 
\xi _{0}^{2} &=&1-\frac{1+\epsilon \cos \theta _{T}}{1+\epsilon } \\
&=&\frac{\epsilon \left( 1-\cos \theta _{T}\right) }{1+\epsilon }
\end{eqnarray}
\end_inset 

 so that 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation} 
\theta _{T}=\arccos \left[ 1-\frac{2\xi _{0}^{2}}{\xi _{0T}^{2}}\right]
\end{equation}
\end_inset 

 and by symmetry 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{eqnarray} 
\theta _{T\min } &=&-\theta _{T} \\
\theta _{T\max } &=&\theta _{T}
\end{eqnarray}
\end_inset 


\layout Standard

Of course, the position of minimum 
\begin_inset Formula \( B\)
\end_inset 

-field is 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation} 
\theta _{0}=0
\end{equation}
\end_inset 


\layout Subsubsection

Bounce Time
\layout Standard

Using (
\begin_inset LatexCommand \ref{g22circ}

\end_inset 

), the normalized bounce time reduces to 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation} 
\lambda \left( \rho ,\xi _{0}\right) =\left[ 1+\left( \frac{R_{0}q\left(
\rho \right) }{a\rho }\right) ^{2}\right] ^{-1/2}\int_{\theta _{\min
}}^{\theta _{\max }}\frac{d\theta }{2\pi }\frac{\xi _{0}}{\xi }\frac{B}{\left| B_{P}\right| }
\end{equation}
\end_inset 

 and, inserting (
\begin_inset LatexCommand \ref{BsBpcirc}

\end_inset 

), which is only a function of 
\begin_inset Formula \( \rho \)
\end_inset 

 and can be taken out of the integral, we get finally
\layout Standard


\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation} 
\lambda \left( \psi \right) =\int_{\theta _{\min }}^{\theta _{\max }}\frac{d\theta }{2\pi }\frac{\xi _{0}}{\xi }
\end{equation}
\end_inset 


\layout Standard

This integral can be performed analytically in a series expansion [dec02thesis]
 and are calculated in Appendix.
