\layout Section

Examples Of Numerical Calculations
\layout Standard

The input data necessary for the numerical calculation of the bounce time
 and other bounce coefficients are the parametrization of the flux-surfaces
 
\begin_inset Formula \( R\left( \psi ,\theta \right) \)
\end_inset 

 and 
\begin_inset Formula \( Z\left( \psi ,\theta \right) \)
\end_inset 

, and the three components of the magnetic field 
\begin_inset Formula \( B_{R}\left( \psi ,\theta \right) \)
\end_inset 

, 
\begin_inset Formula \( B_{Z}\left( \psi ,\theta \right) \)
\end_inset 

 and 
\begin_inset Formula \( B_{\phi }\left( \psi ,\theta \right) 
\)
\end_inset 

.
 These data can be the result of experimental measurements, or simulations.
 They can also be derived analytically for some cases of idealized plasmas.
 In this case, the results of the numerical calculation of the bounce coefficien
t
 can be compared to the analytical calculation of these coefficient, which
 provides a good tool for confirming the analytical formulaes, verifying
 the algorithm, and investigating the numerical precision of the calculation.
 This is the purpose of this section.
\layout Subsection

Circular, Concentric Flux-Surfaces
\layout Subsubsection

constant current profile
\layout Standard

We assume a constant current profile 
\begin_inset Formula \[ 
j\left( \rho \right) =j_{0} 
\]
\end_inset 

 so that the current enclosed in the poloidal surface 
\begin_inset Formula \( S_{P}\left( \rho
\right) \)
\end_inset 

 is 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{eqnarray*} 
I\left( \rho \right) &=&\iint_{S_{P}\left( \rho \right) }j\;dS \\
&=&2\pi a^{2}\int_{0}^{\rho }j\;\rho d\rho \\
&=&\pi a^{2}\rho ^{2}j_{0}
\end{eqnarray*}
\end_inset 

 and the total toroidal current is 
\begin_inset Formula \[ 
I_{0}=I\left( 1\right) =\pi a^{2}j_{0} 
\]
\end_inset 

 so that we can rewrite 
\begin_inset Formula \[ 
I\left( \rho \right) =I_{0}\rho ^{2} 
\]
\end_inset 


\layout Standard

The poloidal magnetic field can be written as (
\begin_inset LatexCommand \ref{Bpcirc}

\end_inset 

) 
\begin_inset Formula \[ 
B_{P}\left( \rho ,\theta \right) =\frac{R_{0}}{R\left( \rho ,\theta \right) }
B_{P0}\left( \rho \right) 
\]
\end_inset 

 with 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation} 
B_{P0}\left( \rho \right) =\frac{\left| \nabla \psi \left( \rho \right)
\right| }{R_{0}}
\end{equation}
\end_inset 


\layout Standard

We find, using Ampere's law, 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{eqnarray*} 
\mu _{0}I\left( \rho \right) &=&\oint B_{P}dl \\
&=&\int_{0}^{2\pi }\rho ad\theta \;\frac{R_{0}}{R}B_{P0}\left( \rho \right)
\\
&=&2\pi \rho aB_{P0}\left( \rho \right) \frac{1}{1+\epsilon }\int_{0}^{2\pi
}\;\frac{d\theta }{2\pi }\frac{1+\epsilon }{1+\epsilon \cos \theta } \\
&=&2\pi \rho aB_{P0}\left( \rho \right) \frac{1}{1+\epsilon }s^{*} \\
&=&\frac{2\pi \rho aB_{P}\left( \rho \right) }{\sqrt{1-\epsilon ^{2}}}
\end{eqnarray*}
\end_inset 

 where we used [dec02thesis] 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation} 
s^{*}\equiv \int_{0}^{2\pi }\;\frac{d\theta }{2\pi }\frac{1+\epsilon }{1+\epsilon \cos \theta }=\sqrt{\frac{1+\epsilon }{1-\epsilon }}
\end{equation}
\end_inset 


\layout Standard

We have then 
\begin_inset Formula \[ 
B_{P0}\left( \rho \right) =B_{Pa}\frac{I\left( \rho \right) }{\rho I_{0}}\sqrt{1-\left( \epsilon _{a}\rho \right) ^{2}} 
\]
\end_inset 

 with 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation} 
\epsilon _{a}=\frac{a}{R_{0}}
\end{equation}
\end_inset 

 and where 
\begin_inset Formula \( B_{Pa}\)
\end_inset 

 is the poloidal field at the edge and above the magnetic axis, given by
 
\begin_inset Formula \[ 
B_{Pa}=\frac{\mu _{0}I_{0}}{2\pi a}=\frac{\mu _{0}aj_{0}}{2} 
\]
\end_inset 

 Note that the factor 
\begin_inset Formula \( \sqrt{1-\epsilon ^{2}}\)
\end_inset 

 is in general neglected in the large aspect ratio approximation 
\begin_inset Formula \( \epsilon _{a}\ll 1\)
\end_inset 

.
\layout Standard

From the expression of the poloidal magnetic field, it is possible to determine
 the poloidal flux 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{eqnarray*} 
\psi \left( \rho ,\theta \right) &=&\frac{1}{2\pi }\iint_{S_{T}\left( \rho
,\theta \right) }B_{P}\left( \rho ,\theta \right) \;dS \\
&=&a\int_{0}^{\rho }RB_{P}\left( \rho ,\theta \right) \;d\rho \\
&=&aR_{0}\int_{0}^{\rho }B_{P0}\left( \rho \right) \;d\rho \\
&=&aR_{0}B_{Pa}\int_{0}^{\rho }\rho \sqrt{1-\left( \epsilon _{a}\rho \right)
^{2}}\;d\rho \\
&=&aR_{0}B_{Pa}\frac{1}{3}\frac{1}{\epsilon _{a}^{2}}\left( 1-\left[
1-\left( \epsilon _{a}\rho \right) ^{2}\right] ^{3/2}\right) \\
&=&\psi _{a}\frac{2}{3}\frac{1}{\epsilon _{a}^{2}}\left( 1-\left[ 1-\left(
\epsilon _{a}\rho \right) ^{2}\right] ^{3/2}\right)
\end{eqnarray*}
\end_inset 

 where we see that 
\begin_inset Formula \( \psi \)
\end_inset 

 is independent of 
\begin_inset Formula \( \theta \)
\end_inset 

 and we define 
\begin_inset Formula \[ 
\psi _{a}=\frac{aR_{0}B_{Pa}}{2}=\frac{\mu _{0}I_{0}R_{0}}{8\pi } 
\]
\end_inset 

 Note that in the large aspect ratio approximation 
\begin_inset Formula \( \epsilon _{a}\rightarrow
0 \)
\end_inset 

, the expression of simplifies to 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation} 
\psi \left( \rho ,\theta \right) =\psi _{a}\rho ^{2}
\end{equation}
\end_inset 


\layout Standard

Note we can also calculate 
\begin_inset Formula \( \psi \)
\end_inset 

 from the general expression of the magnetic field 
\begin_inset Formula \[ 
\mathbf{B}=I\left( \psi \right) \nabla \phi +\nabla \psi \times \nabla \phi 
\]
\end_inset 

 so that (
\begin_inset LatexCommand \ref{Bpcirc}

\end_inset 

) 
\begin_inset Formula \[ 
B_{P0}\left( \rho \right) =\frac{\left| \nabla \psi \right| }{R_{0}}=\frac{1}{R_{0}a}\frac{d\psi }{d\rho } 
\]
\end_inset 

 which is consistent with the calculation above.
\layout Standard

Since the flux-surfaces are circular concentric, il is straightforward to
 calculate 
\begin_inset Formula \( B_{R}\)
\end_inset 

 and 
\begin_inset Formula \( B_{Z}\)
\end_inset 


\begin_inset Formula \begin{eqnarray*} 
B_{R} &=&\mathbf{B}_{p}\cdot \widehat{R}=B_{p}\widehat{\theta }\cdot 
\widehat{R}=-B_{p}\sin \theta \\
B_{Z} &=&\mathbf{B}_{p}\cdot \widehat{Z}=B_{p}\widehat{\theta }\cdot 
\widehat{Z}=B_{p}\cos \theta
\end{eqnarray*}
\end_inset 


\layout Standard

The quantities desired for computation are finally 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{eqnarray*} 
B_{Pa} &=&\frac{\mu _{0}I_{0}}{2\pi a} \\
R\left( \psi ,\theta \right) &=&R_{0}+\rho \cos \theta \\
Z\left( \psi ,\theta \right) &=&Z_{0}+\rho \sin \theta \\
B_{R}\left( \psi ,\theta \right) &=&-B_{p}\sin \theta =-B_{Pa}\rho \frac{R_{0}}{R}\sin \theta \\
B_{Z}\left( \psi ,\theta \right) &=&B_{p}\cos \theta =B_{Pa}\rho \frac{R_{0}}{R}\cos \theta \\
B_{P}\left( \psi ,\theta \right) &=&B_{Pa}\rho \frac{R_{0}}{R} \\
B_{\phi }\left( \psi ,\theta \right) &=&\frac{I\left( \rho \right) }{R} \\
j &=&j_{0}=\frac{2}{\mu _{0}a}B_{Pa} \\
q\left( \psi \right) &=&\rho \left( \psi \right) \frac{a}{R_{0}}\frac{B_{\phi }}{B_{P}}
\end{eqnarray*}
\end_inset 

 and can be expressed as a function of 
\begin_inset Formula \( \left( \psi ,\theta \right) \)
\end_inset 

 as (to order 
\begin_inset Formula \( \epsilon _{a}^{2}\)
\end_inset 

) 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{eqnarray*} 
\rho \left( \psi \right) &=&\sqrt{\frac{\psi }{\psi _{a}}} \\
R\left( \psi ,\theta \right) &=&R_{0}+\sqrt{\frac{\psi }{\psi _{a}}}\cos
\theta \\
Z\left( \psi ,\theta \right) &=&Z_{0}+\sqrt{\frac{\psi }{\psi _{a}}}\sin
\theta \\
B_{R}\left( \psi ,\theta \right) &=&-\frac{2\sqrt{\psi _{a}\psi }}{a}\frac{\sin \theta }{R_{0}+a\sqrt{\psi /\psi _{a}}\cos \theta } \\
B_{Z}\left( \psi ,\theta \right) &=&\frac{2\sqrt{\psi _{a}\psi }}{a}\frac{\cos \theta }{R_{0}+a\sqrt{\psi /\psi _{a}}\cos \theta } \\
B_{P}\left( \psi ,\theta \right) &=&\frac{2\sqrt{\psi _{a}\psi }}{a}\frac{1}{R_{0}+a\sqrt{\psi /\psi _{a}}\cos \theta } \\
B_{\phi }\left( \psi ,\theta \right) &=&\frac{1}{R_{0}+a\sqrt{\psi /\psi _{a}}\cos \theta }I\left( \sqrt{\frac{\psi }{\psi _{a}}}\right) \\
j &=&\frac{4}{\mu _{0}a^{2}R_{0}}\psi _{a} \\
q\left( \psi \right) &=&\frac{a^{2}}{2R_{0}\psi _{a}}I\left( \sqrt{\frac{\psi }{\psi _{a}}}\right)
\end{eqnarray*}
\end_inset 


\layout Subsubsection

parabolic current profile
\layout Standard

We assume here a parabolic current profile 
\begin_inset Formula \[ 
j\left( \rho \right) =j_{0}\left( 1-\rho ^{2}\right) 
\]
\end_inset 

 so that the current enclosed in the poloidal surface 
\begin_inset Formula \( S_{P}\left( \rho
\right) \)
\end_inset 

 is 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{eqnarray*} 
I\left( \rho \right) &=&\iint_{S_{P}\left( \rho \right) }j\;dS \\
&=&2\pi a^{2}\int_{0}^{\rho }j\left( \rho \right) \;\rho d\rho \\
&=&\frac{1}{2}\pi j_{0}a^{2}\rho ^{2}\left[ 2-\rho ^{2}\right]
\end{eqnarray*}
\end_inset 

 and the total toroidal current is 
\begin_inset Formula \[ 
I_{0}=I\left( 1\right) =\frac{1}{2}\pi j_{0}a^{2} 
\]
\end_inset 

 so that we can rewrite 
\begin_inset Formula \[ 
I\left( \rho \right) =I_{0}\rho ^{2}\left[ 2-\rho ^{2}\right] 
\]
\end_inset 


\layout Standard

We still have 
\begin_inset Formula \[ 
B_{P0}\left( \rho \right) =B_{Pa}\frac{I\left( \rho \right) }{\rho I_{0}}\sqrt{1-\left( \frac{\rho a}{R_{0}}\right) ^{2}} 
\]
\end_inset 

 so that 
\begin_inset Formula \[ 
B_{P}\left( \rho \right) =B_{Pa}\rho \left[ 2-\rho ^{2}\right] 
\]
\end_inset 


\layout Standard

From the expression of the poloidal magnetic field, it is again possible
 to determine the poloidal flux 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{eqnarray*} 
\psi \left( \rho ,\theta \right) &=&aR_{0}\int_{0}^{\rho }B_{P0}\left( \rho
\right) \;d\rho \\
&=&aR_{0}B_{Pa}\int_{0}^{\rho }\rho \left[ 2-\rho ^{2}\right] \sqrt{1-\left(
\epsilon _{a}\rho \right) ^{2}}\;d\rho \\
&=&aR_{0}B_{Pa}\left[ 2\int_{0}^{\rho }\rho \sqrt{1-\left( \epsilon _{a}\rho
\right) ^{2}}\;d\rho -\int_{0}^{\rho }\rho ^{3}\sqrt{1-\left( \epsilon
_{a}\rho \right) ^{2}}\;d\rho \right] \\
&=&aR_{0}B_{Pa}\left[ \frac{1}{3\epsilon _{a}^{2}}\left( 2-\left[ 2-\rho
^{2}\right] \left[ 1-\left( \epsilon _{a}\rho \right) ^{2}\right]
^{3/2}\right) -\frac{2}{3\epsilon _{a}^{2}}\int_{0}^{\rho }\rho \left[
1-\left( \epsilon _{a}\rho \right) ^{2}\right] ^{3/2}\;d\rho \right] \\
&=&aR_{0}B_{Pa}\frac{1}{3\epsilon _{a}^{2}}\left[ \left( 2-\left[ 2-\rho
^{2}\right] \left[ 1-\left( \epsilon _{a}\rho \right) ^{2}\right]
^{3/2}\right) -\frac{2}{5}\frac{1}{\epsilon _{a}^{2}}\left( 1-\left[
1-\left( \epsilon _{a}\rho \right) ^{2}\right] ^{5/2}\right) \right] \\
&=&\psi _{a}\frac{4}{9\epsilon _{a}^{2}}\left[ \left( 2-\left[ 2-\rho
^{2}\right] \left[ 1-\left( \epsilon _{a}\rho \right) ^{2}\right]
^{3/2}\right) -\frac{2}{5}\frac{1}{\epsilon _{a}^{2}}\left( 1-\left[
1-\left( \epsilon _{a}\rho \right) ^{2}\right] ^{5/2}\right) \right]
\end{eqnarray*}
\end_inset 

 with 
\begin_inset Formula \[ 
\psi _{a}=\frac{3aR_{0}B_{Pa}}{4} 
\]
\end_inset 


\layout Standard

Note that in the large aspect ratio limit 
\begin_inset Formula \( \epsilon _{a}\rightarrow 0\)
\end_inset 

, this simplifies to 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation} 
\psi \left( \rho ,\theta \right) =\psi _{a}\frac{\rho ^{2}}{3}\left[ 4-\rho
^{2}\right]
\end{equation}
\end_inset 


\the_end
